// **a `contract::failable<E>` that is declared and not answered is the declaration site's mistake, and
// only the declaration site's.**
//
// `failure_type_of` used to read E off the conformance and never ask whether a `failure()` existed at all,
// so whether the declaration was there got discovered by a **name lookup inside AST::GuardLowering**, one
// pass later, in a place with no wording for it - the author saw an unknown-function error about a call they
// had not written.
//
// `AST::UnwrapPlan` carries the declaration beside the type now, so the two answers are one answer. and
// what this file pins is the *deliberate* reading of what happens next: an unanswered conformance makes
// the plan `t_pending`, which is byte for byte what AST::unwrap_plan_for already does for an unanswered
// `unwrap()`. AST::TypeChecker reports the unmet requirement at the `struct`, where the author can act on
// it, and the guard adds nothing about it - a second refusal at the `$e` would be two complaints about one
// mistake.
//
// so the diagnostic that matters is `UnmetInterfaceRequirement` on line 8, naming `failure() : oops&`.
// the two around it are the shape every refused guard has - see `unwrappable_not_declared` and
// `failable_not_declared`, which both carry the same tail.

struct oops
{
    int32 $code;

    constructor(int32 $code) { $this->code = $code; }
}

struct cell : contract::unwrappable<int32>, contract::failable<oops>
{
    private int32 $v;

    constructor(int32 $v) { $this->v = $v; }

    public const function has_value() : bool { return false; }

    public function unwrap() : int32& { return &$this->v; }

    // and no `failure()`. that is the whole of the program
}

function take() : int32
{
    cell $c = cell(1);

    int32 $v = guard $c else ($e) { return $e->code; }

    return $v;
}

echo take();
